<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pillai Lokacharya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1205">1205</a>–<a href="/years/1311">1311</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Vāraṇādrīśa, Lokacharya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pillai Lokacharya, also called Vāraṇādrīśa, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism">Sri Vaishnava</a> leader and philosopher of Tiruvarangam in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> whose works are central to Vishishtadvaita thought. He taught that God's grace is spontaneous and is met by prapatti, a passive surrender, comparing the devotee to a kitten carried in its mother's mouth. His eighteen texts, the Ashtadasa Rahasyangal, mostly in Manipravalam, include the Srivachana Bhushana of 484 sentences, and his teachings shaped the Tenkalai school. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a>'s invasion struck Tiruvarangam he left the town to protect the image of the Aranganatha temple, and he died in 1311 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pillai-lokacharya.jpg" alt="Pillai Lokacharya" width="330" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Debanjon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swami_Pillai_Lokacharya.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the account of a Sri Vaishnava temple chronicle, when Ulugh Khan, the future Muhammad bin Tughluq, sacked the temple of Srirangam in 1323, some 12,000 scholars and temple functionaries were killed; the teacher <b>Pillai Lokacharya</b> carried the processional image south, and Vedanta Desika hid the sacred manuscripts.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
